<PageLayout title="Export Qwen Chat (chat.qwen.ai)" description="Back up your Qwen Chat consumer conversations to local Markdown with TotalRecalls." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Guides", href: "/guides/" }, { label: "Qwen" }]}>
  <h1>Export Qwen Chat (chat.qwen.ai)</h1>
  <p>TotalRecalls reads your active Qwen Chat (chat.qwen.ai) session and exports every conversation to local Markdown and JSON.</p>

  <h2>Steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="https://chat.qwen.ai">chat.qwen.ai</a> and sign in.</li>
    <li>Launch TotalRecalls. Select <strong>"Qwen Chat"</strong> as the provider.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Scan Chats</strong>. TotalRecalls reads your session cookie from the browser profile.</li>
    <li>Choose which chats to export.</li>
    <li>Select format: <strong>Markdown + JSON</strong> and pick a destination folder.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Export</strong>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Consumer product only.</strong> TotalRecalls targets the Qwen Chat consumer product at <code>chat.qwen.ai</code>, not Alibaba's DashScope / Bailian / Model Studio developer APIs.</li>
    <li><strong>Alibaba ToS compliance.</strong> We never call DashScope endpoints or use Alibaba API keys. We read your consumer chat history the same way a backup tool would read a webmail inbox — from your own browser session.</li>
    <li><strong>Why two products?</strong> Alibaba ships Qwen two ways: a consumer chat at <code>chat.qwen.ai</code> (which is what users want to back up) and a developer model API at DashScope (which has its own ToS rules). TotalRecalls only touches the former.</li>
  </ul>
</PageLayout>
